Your app.js is well organized. There are no critical architectural problems. Most of my recommendations are about production hardening and keeping the file maintainable as the project grows.

✅ What You Did Well
1. Configuration Order
require("dotenv").config();
Correct.

Environment variables should always be loaded before anything that depends on them.

2. Middleware Order
I like the sequence:

app.use(express.urlencoded(...));
app.use(express.json());
app.use(express.static(...));
Then session.

Then res.locals.

Then routes.

This is the correct order.

3. Session Storage
store: MongoStore.create({
    mongoUrl: process.env.MONGODB_URI
})
Excellent.

Using MongoDB for sessions is much better than the default in-memory session store.

4. res.locals.user
res.locals.user = req.session.user || null;
Excellent.

Now every EJS page automatically knows who the logged-in user is.

That keeps your templates clean.

5. Route Organization
/auth
/
/predictions
/premium
Very clean.

6. Local Email Module
I like this.

const sendTheEmail = require("./email");
Much better than putting Nodemailer logic directly inside app.js.

Things I'd Improve
1. Remove Unused Imports
I noticed:

const nodemailer = require("nodemailer");
But you aren't using it in app.js.

The email logic has already been moved into email.js.

So remove:

const nodemailer = require("nodemailer");
This is a small cleanup, but it's good practice.

2. Session Cookie
Currently:

cookie: {
    maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24
}
For development, that's fine.

For production, I'd expand it:

cookie: {
    maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24,
    httpOnly: true,
    secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
    sameSite: "lax"
}
This makes cookies more secure.

3. Add a Global 404 Handler
Right now, if someone visits:

/thispagedoesnotexist
Express will return its default response.

Instead, after all your routes, add:

app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });
});
This gives users a consistent experience.

4. Add a Global Error Handler
Since your controllers already call:

next(err);
you should have one centralized error handler at the end:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.error(err);

    res.status(500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error"
    });
});
This completes your error pipeline.

5. Logging
Instead of:

console.log(error);
I'd use:

console.error(error);
Errors belong on the error stream.

Later, we can introduce a logging library if you need structured logs.

6. Separate Contact Routes
Eventually, I would move:

app.post("/send")
and

app.post("/booking")
into a dedicated routes/controller pair.

For example:

routes/contactRoutes.js
controllers/contactController.js
That keeps app.js focused on configuration rather than request handling.

This isn't urgent, but it's a good next step as the project grows.

Security Review
The one thing I noticed is that the email HTML is built directly from user input:

<p>${message}</p>
If that content is ever displayed elsewhere, consider escaping or sanitizing it. Since it's currently being sent as an email to you, the risk is much lower, but it's something to keep in mind if you reuse the same data in the application.

Performance
No concerns.

Static files are served correctly.

Session configuration is appropriate.

Routes are organized.

Architecture
At this point, your application has a nice layered structure:

app.js
    │
    ▼
Routes
    │
    ▼
Middleware
    │
    ▼
Controllers
    │
    ▼
Models
    │
    ▼
MongoDB
That's exactly the kind of architecture we aim for in an Express application.

My Recommended Next Changes
Before adding more features, I'd make these four improvements:

Remove the unused nodemailer import from app.js.

Add a global 404 handler after all routes.

Add a centralized error-handling middleware (app.use((err, req, res, next) => { ... })) at the very end.

Harden the session cookie with httpOnly, sameSite, and secure (conditionally for production).

Once those are in place, your application's infrastructure will be much more resilient.

Overall Assessment
When we first started reviewing the project, there were a few structural and security issues. As we've worked through each file, those have largely been resolved. Now, the remaining work is about polish and production readiness rather than fixing the foundation.

I'd estimate the project is around 85–90% of the way to a solid production-ready Express application. The remaining work consists of:

Centralized validation

