What this means
When you write a route like this:

router.get("/login", authController.login);
Express expects authController.login to be a function.

Instead, it's receiving an object.

This usually happens for one of these reasons:

Possibility 1 (Most common)
You're importing the controller incorrectly.

For example:

const authController = require("../controllers/authController");
is correct if authController.js exports functions like:

exports.login = ...
exports.signup = ...
But if you accidentally did something like:

module.exports = {
    login: {...}   // ❌ object instead of function
}
or imported the wrong file, you'll get this error.

Possibility 2
You accidentally called the function instead of passing it:

router.get("/login", authController.login());
❌ Wrong.

It should be:

router.get("/login", authController.login);
Possibility 3
One of the route handlers doesn't exist.

Example:

router.get("/about", authController.about);
but in your controller:

exports.about = {
    pageTitle: "About"
};
instead of

exports.about = (req, res) => {
    ...
};
