There it is! 👏 I can see the bug immediately.

Inside your loop, you wrote:

<% predictions.forEach(function(post){ %>
That means the current prediction is stored in the variable post.

But later you wrote:

<% if(prediction.expired){ %>
There is no variable named prediction in this scope. The only variable is post.

That's why EJS throws:

ReferenceError: prediction is not defined
But there's a second issue
Even if you change it to:

<% if(post.expired){ %>
it still won't work, because your Prediction schema does not have an expired field.

Earlier, your schema included fields like:

kickoffTime
status
prediction
analysis
...
There was no:

expired: Boolean
The correct solution
If your goal is to show Expired when the kickoff time has already passed, compare the dates instead:

Replace this:

<p>
    <% if(prediction.expired){ %>

        Expired

    <% } %>
</p>
with:

<p>
    <% if (new Date(post.kickoffTime) < new Date()) { %>

        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Expired</span>

    <% } else { %>

        <span class="badge bg-success">Upcoming</span>

    <% } %>
</p>
Or, if you're using the status field
Since your model already has:

status: "Pending" | "Won" | "Lost" | "Void"
you could display that instead:

<p>
    <strong>Status:</strong>

    <span class="badge bg-info">
        <%= post.status %>
    </span>
</p>
That avoids introducing another concept (expired) if you don't need it.

One more improvement
I also noticed you're doing:

<%= post.createdBy ? post.createdBy.username : "Unknown" %>
This will only show the username if your query populates createdBy, for example:

const predictions = await Prediction.find({ isPremium: false })
    .populate("createdBy", "username");
If you haven't added .populate(), you'll always see "Unknown" because createdBy will just be an ObjectId.

