An EJS variable typo crashed the page.

Every error resulted in a long stack trace instead of a user-friendly page.

That's exactly what a global error-handling layer is designed to address.

I propose we stick to this roadmap
Phase 3 — Production Hardening
Step 1 — Error Pages
Create:

views/
    403.ejs
    404.ejs
    500.ejs
These will give users professional pages instead of ugly stack traces.

Step 2 — 404 Middleware
At the very bottom of app.js (after all routes):

app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });
});
Step 3 — Global Error Handler
Immediately after the 404 handler:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.error(err);

    res.status(err.status || 500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error",
        error: process.env.NODE_ENV === "development" ? err : null
    });
});
Notice how your controllers already do this:

catch (err) {
    next(err);
}
That means they're already prepared to use a global error handler.

Step 4 — 403 Page
Whenever authorization fails:

return res.status(403).render("403", {
    pageTitle: "Access Denied"
});
instead of sending plain text.

