You've successfully added the 404 and global error middleware, 
but there is one important issue with their placement.

❌ Problem: Middleware order
Right now, your code is:

app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

app.use("/", homeRoutes);

app.use("/predictions", predictionRoutes);
app.use("/premium", premiumRoutes);

// 404
app.use((req, res) => {
    ...
});

// 500
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    ...
});

// POST routes
app.post("/send", ...);

app.post("/booking", ...);
This means every request that isn't handled by the routes above will hit the 404 middleware first, 
so Express will never reach:

app.post("/send", ...)
or

app.post("/booking", ...)
because the 404 handler ends the request.

✅ Correct order
Your application should follow this flow:

Middleware
        ↓
Routes
        ↓
404 Handler
        ↓
500 Error Handler
The 404 and 500 handlers should be the last middleware in the entire application.

So move them to the bottom, after all your routes:

app.use("/auth", authRoutes);
app.use("/", homeRoutes);
app.use("/predictions", predictionRoutes);
app.use("/premium", premiumRoutes);

app.post("/send", ...);

app.post("/booking", ...);

// 404 Handler
app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });
});

// Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.error(err);

    res.status(err.status || 500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error",
        error: process.env.NODE_ENV === "development" ? err : null
    });
});
Another improvement
Your global error handler is already good:

console.error(err);
I'd make it slightly more informative:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {

    console.error("Global Error Handler");
    console.error(err.stack);

    res.status(err.status || 500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error",
        error: process.env.NODE_ENV === "development" ? err : null
    });

});
That makes debugging much easier.

