where dis we add the 404 middleware, and what did we add?

Great question. Understanding why we added it is just as important as knowing where.

Where do we add the 404 middleware?
It belongs in app.js, after every route and before the global error handler.

The correct order is:

Express Middleware
        ↓
Session Middleware
        ↓
Application Routes
        ↓
POST Routes (/send, /booking)
        ↓
404 Middleware
        ↓
Global Error Middleware
So your app.js should end like this:

app.use("/auth", authRoutes);

app.use("/", homeRoutes);

app.use("/predictions", predictionRoutes);

app.use("/premium", premiumRoutes);

app.post("/send", async (req, res) => {
    ...
});

app.post("/booking", async (req, res) => {
    ...
});

// 404 Middleware
app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });
});

// Global Error Middleware
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.error(err);

    res.status(err.status || 500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error",
        error: process.env.NODE_ENV === "development" ? err : null
    });
});
What is the 404 middleware?
This is the code we added:

app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });
});
Let's break it down.

app.use(...)
This middleware runs only if Express hasn't already sent a response.

That means:

No route matched the request.

No earlier middleware handled it.

For example, if someone visits:

http://localhost:4000/football
and you don't have:

router.get("/football", ...)
Express continues searching through your routes. When it reaches the 404 middleware, it knows nothing matched.

res.status(404)
This sets the HTTP status code to 404 Not Found.

Without it, Express would return 200 OK, 
which would incorrectly tell browsers and search engines that the page exists.

.render("404")
Instead of sending plain text like:

Cannot GET /football
Express renders your custom views/404.ejs page.

That's why we created that file.

What happens behind the scenes?
Suppose a user visits:

http://localhost:4000/abcxyz
Express checks:

/auth
❌ No match.

Then:

/
❌ No match.

Then:

/Predictions
❌ No match.

Then:

/premium
❌ No match.

Then:

/send
❌ No match.

Then:

/booking
❌ No match.

Finally, Express reaches:

app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404");
});
and displays your custom "Page Not Found" page.

Why did we add it?
Before adding this middleware, if a route didn't exist, 
Express would respond with its default message, such as:

Cannot GET /abcxyz
That works for development but looks unprofessional to users.

After adding the middleware, visitors see your branded 404 page, which is a much better experience.

A quick check
Once you've moved the 404 middleware to the bottom of app.js, test it by opening:

http://localhost:4000/thispagedoesnotexist
If everything is wired correctly, your custom 404.ejs page should appear instead of Express's 
default "Cannot GET ..." message.

That's the simplest way to confirm the middleware is working exactly as intended.


